Subject: ext4_super.c uses memcpy and does not include string.h

ext4_sb_write_trans() -- added by patch 0023 -- calls memcpy. The file
never included <string.h>, and on macOS it builds anyway because Apple's
headers pull the declaration in through something else.

glibc does not:

    ext4_super.c:202: error: implicit declaration of function 'memcpy'

which is the first thing that stops this tree compiling on Linux, and it
would stop it compiling on any host whose headers are tidier than Apple's.
An implicit declaration is also an int-returning one, so on a system where
it merely warned, the pointer would be truncated.

Found while porting the tools to Linux, so that the oracle suites can run
against the real Linux ext4 on a CI runner rather than only inside Docker
on a developer's Mac.

--- a/src/ext4_super.c
+++ b/src/ext4_super.c
@@ -50,6 +50,11 @@
 #include <ext4_bcache.h>
 #include <ext4_crc32.h>
 
+/* memcpy, used by ext4_sb_write_trans below. Apple's headers pull this in
+ * through something else; glibc does not, and the file does not build on
+ * Linux without it. */
+#include <string.h>
+
 uint32_t ext4_block_group_cnt(struct ext4_sblock *s)
 {
 	uint64_t blocks_count = ext4_sb_get_blocks_cnt(s);
